WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:01.380
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Understanding the sentiment

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:03.547
and meaning behind language
requires an architecture

00:00:03.547 --> 00:00:05.310
that can handle a lot of data.

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:07.050
Join us as we talk
to Mito.ai about how

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:08.860
they handle their data
processing on this episode

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:09.630
of "Stack Chat."

00:00:12.795 --> 00:00:14.420
Thank you so much
for joining us today.

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:16.378
Can you tell us a little
bit more about Mito.ai

00:00:16.378 --> 00:00:17.100
and what you do?

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.600
MARIT RODEVAND: Yeah, so we
help large corporations increase

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.630
sales by finding insights
and sales triggers

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:26.690
about their existing customers
and potential prospects,

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:28.870
and we do this by
analyzing large amounts

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:30.750
of unstructured data
on the internet.

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:32.500
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
what's the technology

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.540
behind how you make your AI?

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:37.490
MARIT RODEVAND: We need
to do a lot of things

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:39.180
with all of this information.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:44.110
The first thing is detecting--
or separating apple from apple.

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:47.230
People think that's trivial,
but it's actually quite hard.

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:50.510
And we do it in a
language-agnostic manner,

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:53.230
which means that, through
our data-processing pipeline,

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:55.270
we can process any
language, and being

00:00:55.270 --> 00:01:00.010
able to do this for all very
difficult cases, not just apple

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:02.800
and apple, but similar ones
in Norwegian and Swedish

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:03.370
and German.

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:06.850
And that's also what makes
our technology unique,

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:10.300
this combination of having a
language-agnostic knowledge

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:13.510
graph, and on top of
that one, building a lot

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:16.390
of machine learning
modules to solve all

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:18.610
these really hard questions.

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:23.320
And we are using Apache Spark
as our main data processing

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:27.605
platform, which Patrick can
talk more in detail about.

00:01:27.605 --> 00:01:29.980
PATRICK SKJENNUM: So once the
Spark Streaming application

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:32.340
has pulled all of its
content from Pub/Sub,

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:36.020
it processes it through
our NLP pipeline.

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:37.770
This is where we do
the entity extraction,

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:39.612
we cluster the content,
and we interact

00:01:39.612 --> 00:01:41.320
with all of these
machine learning models

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:42.700
that we have trained.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.060
These models, they are
trained in Apache Spark

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.260
as well, in a batch
processing unit,

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:50.440
which runs on ephemeral clusters
with preemptive nodes, which

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:52.840
is awesome, because then we
can just spin up 1,000 nodes,

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:55.390
and we can train a
really expensive model

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:59.720
for a short time and not
have to leave them running.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:02.560
And once we have done
that, the last step

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.200
in the stream of
processing pipeline

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.720
is to index the data back
into the knowledge graph.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:09.610
And for that, we're
using Elasticsearch,

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:12.970
which is also a really
awesome piece of software.

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:16.030
It's got really great
searching capabilities, so

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:19.330
you we search for "apples"
and get hits for "apple,"

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:21.250
and that sort of thing.

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:25.030
And once the data is
indexed into Elasticsearch,

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:29.720
it is made searchable from our
APIs, which run in GraphQL.

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:34.300
These APIs run in a microservice
architecture in Kubernetes.

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:36.460
So that is kind of
the ecosystem that

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:38.050
interacts with all
of the data that

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:40.240
is processed in Apache Spark.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:42.820
MARIT RODEVAND: We make this
API accessible to our customers,

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:44.487
because that's how
they integrate, also,

00:02:44.487 --> 00:02:46.450
with our service,
taking data from us

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:50.530
and into their internal CRM
and prospecting systems.

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:54.250
And we've even open-sourced a
programming language in order

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:58.040
to customize APIs in
GraphQL very easily.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:01.097
So people should check that
out if they want to do that.

00:03:01.097 --> 00:03:03.680
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So obviously,
over the last couple of years,

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.555
you've learned a ton
about your architecture.

00:03:05.555 --> 00:03:07.097
If you could go back
and start again,

00:03:07.097 --> 00:03:08.847
what would you take
with you that you felt

00:03:08.847 --> 00:03:10.120
was the most important thing?

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:11.578
PATRICK SKJENNUM:
One takeaway here

00:03:11.578 --> 00:03:13.790
is that we're using Apache
Spark quite heavily,

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:15.998
and that means that we have
to configure Apache Spark

00:03:15.998 --> 00:03:16.940
clusters.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:19.870
And if I would get a
dollar for every hour

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:21.640
I spend configuring
Spark clusters,

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:22.690
I would be a rich man.

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:24.940
Configuring Spark
clusters is not

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:26.620
something that should
be underestimated.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:28.402
You need to configure
the topology,

00:03:28.402 --> 00:03:30.610
you need to configure how
many nodes you should have,

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:32.715
and all that sort of things.

00:03:32.715 --> 00:03:34.090
What you do get
from Spark though

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:37.480
is all of these machine
learning libraries and a lot

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:39.860
of utilities in this ecosystem.

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:42.910
But we could have
utilized those either way,

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:48.340
by using other kinds of systems,
either in Python or likewise.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:51.760
So one obvious thing that we,
perhaps, should consider more

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:55.210
is utilizing managed
services like Dataflow,

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:58.150
where you can use this
managed service that

00:03:58.150 --> 00:03:59.590
can do all of the
transformations,

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:02.770
and then you can use external
APIs to do the machine learning

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:03.700
stuff.

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:06.100
And then you would also
decouple the system

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:09.670
and have the data processing
in one place and the machine

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:13.270
learning contained
in other services.

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:16.329
So when we started off, we
had a single messaging queue

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:18.740
where we published
all of the content.

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:22.029
This meant that if either
that queue went down

00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:24.820
or some other service
interacting with a queue

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:27.242
went down, we were
clogging the queue.

00:04:27.242 --> 00:04:29.200
We would have a backlog
or a lot of things that

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:30.970
would have to be processed.

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:33.690
And that wasn't sustainable, at
least not when, the customers,

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:35.830
they require that the
delay through our system

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:37.580
is as low as possible.

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:40.840
So as a solution to that,
we swapped over to Pub/Sub

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:43.000
and created this
priority concept

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.640
where you, using
machine learning,

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:48.940
can determine which
priority queue

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:50.980
should this content be put on.

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:54.010
And that is based on
feedback from our customers,

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:56.022
either directly
through our API, where

00:04:56.022 --> 00:04:57.730
the customers can
choose what is relevant

00:04:57.730 --> 00:04:59.170
and what is not relevant.

00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:02.380
And based on this, we can infer
what content matters the most

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:03.800
to our customers.

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:05.350
And based on that,
we can utilize

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:09.480
this to route the content
through the right queues, such

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:12.282
that, for instance, a
stock market update is

00:05:12.282 --> 00:05:13.990
put on the high-priority
queue because we

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:17.295
know that our customers would
want that as soon as possible.

00:05:17.295 --> 00:05:19.670
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Thank you
so much for joining us today.

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:20.480
MARIT RODEVAND:
Thanks for having us.

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:22.540
PATRICK SKJENNUM: Yeah,
thanks for having us.

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:24.332
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Get
started with Dataflow

00:05:24.332 --> 00:05:26.552
using these templates
and tutorials.

00:05:26.552 --> 00:05:28.510
Thanks for watching, and
make sure to subscribe

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:30.700
for more great Google
Cloud Platform content.

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:33.030
We'll see you next
time on "Stack Chat."

